      "math_academy_id": "q-87276",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/642590d3e04de8365075f79f7954c5123019326b1f4addc618a9a2f54a09e881.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(x-1)+3?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/da5e6848776232c4f4429c8b3c5e3fdaaae2e9a99a180dfe409893e97d407036.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(x-1)+3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/8188a686d0f9c579f44601645667c0209a39b6500864eb9479147940ef1ebb26.png)",
